//? Title: Quantile Regression: Modelling the Whole Conditional Distribution
//? Subtitle: The check loss, the linear program and its dual, an exact simplex against ADMM, and standard errors, derived, checked by hand and by simulation, and applied to New York ozone, using datamunge's stats::QuantileRegression
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Least squares estimates how the mean of a response changes with the predictors. Quantile regression
estimates how any chosen quantile changes: the 10th percentile, the median, the 90th. This report
derives the estimator from the check loss, writes it as a linear program and derives its dual. It
then solves the program with an exact simplex, which the library now uses by default, and with
ADMM. Hand examples show the counting rule behind a sample quantile, and the ten candidate lines
through five points. They also show that the optimal line is a step function of \(\tau\), and that it is
not always unique. On a cloud whose spread fans out with \(x\), the fitted slopes rise from 1.37 at \(\tau=0.1\) to 2.79 at
\(\tau=0.9\), against true values of 1.36 and 2.64, while least squares reports only the middle. One extreme
response moves the least-squares slope from 1.50 to 131 and leaves the median slope at exactly 1.50.
A point of high leverage breaks both. The theory checks out to rounding: exactly \(n\tau-1\) points lie
below each fit, and the primal and dual objectives agree. The simplex needs 5 to 8 pivots for up
to 20 000 rows. ADMM stops at its 10 000-iteration cap from 1000 rows up, with losses \(2\times10^{-9}\) to \(4\times10^{-8}\) above the
optimum. In
500 simulated samples the IID standard error is 16–17% too small and its 95% intervals cover 86–89%
of the time; the NID sandwich and the bootstrap cover 94–96%. On the New York ozone data a degree
Fahrenheit raises the 10th percentile of ozone by 1.4 ppb, the median by 2.1 and the 75th
percentile by 2.5. The library also gained the dual solution and IID, NID and bootstrap standard
errors, plus a quantile-process helper. A thin QR replaced a factorisation whose memory grew as \(n^2\).
Across 120 fits with ties, the simplex reached scikit-learn's linear-programming optimum to
\(10^{-15}\).
)

\toc

> The problem

Least squares fits the conditional *mean* \(\mathbb E[Y\mid X=x]\). Often the question is about another part
of the distribution. A drug may help the sickest patients more than the typical patient. Peak ozone,
not average ozone, breaks the health standard. The spread of the response may grow with the
predictor, so that one line through the middle hides how the tails move. Quantile regression
(Koenker and Bassett, 1978) fits the conditional quantile

$$
Q_Y(\tau\mid x)=\inf\{y:\Pr(Y\le y\mid X=x)\ge\tau\},\qquad 0<\tau<1,
$$
\alttext(Q sub Y of tau given x equals the infimum of y such that the probability that Y is at most y given X equals x is at least tau, for tau between 0 and 1.)

as a linear function \(x^\top\beta(\tau)\), one \(\tau\) at a time. The median is \(\tau=\tfrac12\). Fitting a grid of
values of \(\tau\) describes the whole conditional distribution without assuming normal errors or a
constant variance. This report derives the estimator and the linear program behind it, solves that
program two ways, derives and checks the standard errors, and applies the method to the New York
ozone data.

> The method

>> The check loss and the sample quantile

The mean of a sample minimises the sum of squared deviations. The \(\tau\)-quantile minimises a sum of
asymmetric absolute deviations,

$$
\hat q_\tau=\arg\min_q\sum_{i=1}^n\rho_\tau(y_i-q),\qquad
\rho_\tau(r)=r\bigl(\tau-\mathbf 1\{r<0\}\bigr)=\begin{cases}\tau r,&r\ge0,\\(\tau-1)r,&r<0.\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(q hat tau is the argmin over q of the sum over i of rho tau of y i minus q, where the check loss rho tau of r equals r times tau minus the indicator that r is negative: tau r for r at least 0, and tau minus 1 times r for r negative.)

A point above the fit costs \(\tau\) per unit and a point below costs \(1-\tau\). The objective is convex
and piecewise linear in \(q\), with a corner at every \(y_i\). Moving \(q\) up by a small amount raises the
cost of the points at or below \(q\) and lowers that of the points above it, so the right and left
derivatives are

$$
D^+(q)=(1-\tau)N_\le(q)-\tau\bigl(n-N_\le(q)\bigr)=N_\le(q)-n\tau,\qquad D^-(q)=N_<(q)-n\tau,
$$
\alttext(The right derivative D plus of q equals 1 minus tau times N at most q, minus tau times n minus N at most q, which is N at most q minus n tau. The left derivative D minus of q equals N less than q minus n tau.)

where \(N_\le(q)\) and \(N_<(q)\) count the observations at most \(q\) and below \(q\). A minimiser needs
\(D^-(q)\le0\le D^+(q)\), that is

$$
N_<(q)\le n\tau\le N_\le(q).
$$
\alttext(N less than q is at most n tau, which is at most N at most q.)

At most a fraction \(\tau\) of the sample lies below \(q\) and at least a fraction \(\tau\) lies at or below it:
\(q\) is a sample \(\tau\)-quantile. When \(n\tau\) is not an integer the minimiser is the unique order statistic
\(y_{(\lceil n\tau\rceil)}\). When it is an integer, every \(q\) between \(y_{(n\tau)}\) and \(y_{(n\tau+1)}\) is a minimiser. With
weights \(w_i\) the counts become sums of weights.

>> Regression quantiles

Replacing the constant \(q\) by \(x_i^\top\beta\) gives the regression quantile

$$
\hat\beta(\tau)=\arg\min_{\beta\in\mathbb R^p}\sum_{i=1}^nw_i\rho_\tau\bigl(y_i-x_i^\top\beta\bigr).
$$
\alttext(beta hat of tau is the argmin over beta in R p of the sum over i of w i times rho tau of y i minus x i transpose beta.)

The population version justifies it. If \(Q_Y(\tau\mid x)=x^\top\beta(\tau)\), the derivative of
\(\mathbb E\,\rho_\tau(Y-x^\top b)\) in \(b\) is \(\mathbb E\bigl[x\bigl(\Pr(Y<x^\top b\mid x)-\tau\bigr)\bigr]\), which vanishes at \(b=\beta(\tau)\), and the
objective is convex. Nothing is assumed about the shape of the errors or about how their spread
depends on \(x\). Two properties follow from the definition. The fit is *equivariant*: replacing \(y\) by
\(ay+X\gamma\) with \(a>0\) gives \(a\hat\beta(\tau)+\gamma\). And conditional quantiles commute with increasing
transformations, \(Q_{h(Y)}(\tau\mid x)=h(Q_Y(\tau\mid x))\), which conditional means do not.

>> A linear program and its dual

Split each residual into its positive and negative parts, \(y_i-x_i^\top\beta=u_i-v_i\) with \(u_i,v_i\ge0\). The fit
is the linear program

$$
\min_{\beta,u,v}\;\sum_iw_i\bigl(\tau u_i+(1-\tau)v_i\bigr)\quad\text{subject to}\quad X\beta+u-v=y,\;u\ge0,\;v\ge0.
$$
\alttext(Minimise over beta, u and v the sum over i of w i times tau u i plus 1 minus tau times v i, subject to X beta plus u minus v equals y, with u and v non-negative.)

With a multiplier \(w_ia_i\) for row \(i\) of the constraint, the Lagrangian is
\(\sum_iw_i\bigl[(\tau-a_i)u_i+(1-\tau+a_i)v_i+a_iy_i\bigr]-\beta^\top X^\top Wa\). It is bounded below in \(\beta\) only if
\(X^\top Wa=0\), and in \(u\) and \(v\) only if \(\tau-1\le a_i\le\tau\). The dual is therefore

$$
\max_a\;\sum_iw_ia_iy_i\quad\text{subject to}\quad X^\top Wa=0,\quad\tau-1\le a_i\le\tau .
$$
\alttext(Maximise over a the sum over i of w i a i y i, subject to X transpose W a equal to zero and tau minus 1 at most a i at most tau.)

Complementary slackness says \(a_i=\tau\) for a point above the fit and \(a_i=\tau-1\) for a point below it;
only points on the fit take values in between. At the optimum the two objectives are equal. The
shifted values \(a_i+1-\tau\in[0,1]\) are Koenker's *regression rank scores*.

A linear program attains its optimum at a vertex. Here a vertex is a set \(h\) of \(p\) rows with an
invertible submatrix \(X_h\), and \(\hat\beta=X_h^{-1}y_h\): the fitted plane passes exactly through \(p\) of the
points. The counting rule for the sample quantile carries over. Write \(a_i=\tau-b_i\), with \(b_i=1\) below
the fit, \(b_i=0\) above it and \(b_i\in[0,1]\) on it. With an intercept, the first row of \(X^\top a=0\) (unit
weights) is \(\sum_ia_i=n\tau-N_--\sum_{\text{on}}b_i=0\), so

$$
N_-\le n\tau\le N_-+N_0 ,
$$
\alttext(N minus, the number of points below the fit, is at most n tau, which is at most N minus plus N zero, the number of points on the fit.)

where \(N_-\) and \(N_0\) count the points below and on the fit. With \(N_0=p\), the share of points below the
fitted plane is within \(p/n\) of \(\tau\).

>> An exact simplex

The library's default solver walks from vertex to vertex. At a vertex \(h\) with residuals \(r=y-X\hat\beta\),
let \(d_j=X_h^{-1}e_j\). Moving to \(\hat\beta+td_j\) keeps every basis row except \(h_j\) on the fit, and moves the fit at
row \(h_j\) by \(t\). Along that edge the loss is

$$
f_j(t)=\sum_iw_i\rho_\tau(r_i-tc_i),\qquad c_i=x_i^\top d_j ,
$$
\alttext(f j of t equals the sum over i of w i rho tau of r i minus t c i, where c i equals x i transpose d j.)

convex and piecewise linear, with a corner at each \(t_i=r_i/c_i\). As \(t\to-\infty\) its slope is
\(-\sum_iw_i\lvert c_i\rvert\kappa_i\), with \(\kappa_i=\tau\) if \(c_i>0\) and \(1-\tau\) otherwise, and crossing corner \(t_i\) raises the
slope by \(w_i\lvert c_i\rvert\). The minimiser is the first corner, in sorted order, at which the slope turns
non-negative: a weighted quantile of the \(t_i\), found by one sort. The solver evaluates every edge,
takes the one that lowers the loss most, swaps the row at its minimising corner into the basis,
and repeats.

It stops when no edge lowers the loss, and that is a proof of optimality. Using \(X^\top Wa=0\), the right and
left derivatives of \(f_j\) at \(t=0\) are \(w_{h_j}(a_{h_j}+1-\tau)\) and \(w_{h_j}(a_{h_j}-\tau)\). Neither edge direction
descends exactly when \(\tau-1\le a_{h_j}\le\tau\), the dual feasibility of the vertex. The basis duals solve
\(X_h^\top W_ha_h=-\sum_{i\notin h}w_ia_ix_i\), with \(a_i\) fixed by the sign of \(r_i\) off the basis.

Two details make this robust. The start is the \(p\) linearly independent rows nearest the
least-squares fit, so that few pivots are needed. Ties in the data, such as rounded responses or
repeated rows, put more than \(p\) points on a fit. At such a *degenerate* vertex an edge can have zero
length, and the descent can stall short of the optimum. The solver therefore walks on responses
perturbed by a fixed pseudo-random amount of relative size \(10^{-9}\), which removes the ties. At the
final basis it recomputes \(\hat\beta\) from the unperturbed responses and certifies it: the basis duals must
lie in \([\tau-1,\tau]\), and every point clearly off the fit must have the sign its dual value claims. One
pivot costs \(O(pn\log n)\).

>> ADMM

The alternating direction method of multipliers (Boyd et al., 2011) is the alternative. It splits off
the residual, \(\min\sum_iw_i\rho_\tau(r_i)\) subject to \(y-X\beta-r=0\), and with a penalty \(\varrho>0\) and scaled dual \(u\)
iterates

$$
\beta\leftarrow\arg\min_\beta\lVert y-r+u-X\beta\rVert_2^2,\qquad
r_i\leftarrow\operatorname{prox}_i(y_i-x_i^\top\beta+u_i),\qquad
u\leftarrow u+y-X\beta-r .
$$
\alttext(beta is updated to the least-squares solution of y minus r plus u against X; each r i to the proximal map of y i minus x i transpose beta plus u i; and u to u plus y minus X beta minus r.)

The first step is least squares with a fixed design, done with one thin QR factorisation. The second
minimises \(w_i\rho_\tau(r)+\tfrac\varrho2(r-v)^2\), whose solution is an asymmetric soft threshold:
\(r=v-w_i\tau/\varrho\) if \(v>w_i\tau/\varrho\), \(r=v+w_i(1-\tau)/\varrho\) if \(v<-w_i(1-\tau)/\varrho\), and \(r=0\) in between. At a fixed point,
\(\varrho u_i\) is a subgradient of \(w_i\rho_\tau\) at \(r_i\), so \(\varrho u_i/w_i\) estimates the dual \(a_i\). Each iteration costs \(O(np)\), but
convergence on this non-smooth problem is slow, and the answer is only as accurate as the
stopping tolerances.

>> Standard errors

Under regularity conditions (Koenker, 2005, section 4.2),

$$
\sqrt n\bigl(\hat\beta(\tau)-\beta(\tau)\bigr)\xrightarrow{d}N\bigl(0,\;\tau(1-\tau)H^{-1}JH^{-1}\bigr),\qquad
J=\mathbb E[xx^\top],\quad H=\mathbb E\bigl[f_x(x^\top\beta(\tau))\,xx^\top\bigr],
$$
\alttext(Root n times beta hat of tau minus beta of tau converges in distribution to a normal with mean zero and covariance tau times 1 minus tau times H inverse J H inverse, where J is the expectation of x x transpose and H the expectation of f x at x transpose beta of tau times x x transpose.)

where \(f_x\) is the conditional density of \(Y\) given \(x\). A sketch: the estimator nearly solves
\(n^{-1/2}\sum_ix_i(\tau-\mathbf 1\{y_i<x_i^\top\hat\beta\})=0\). The summands at the true \(\beta\) have mean zero and covariance \(\tau(1-\tau)J\),
and a small change \(\delta\) in \(\beta\) shifts their mean by \(-H\delta\), since \(\Pr(Y<x^\top(\beta+\delta)\mid x)\approx\tau+f_x\,x^\top\delta\).
Solving for \(\delta\) gives the sandwich. The density at the quantile is unknown and is estimated in one of
three ways.

- *IID.* If the errors are independent of \(x\), \(f_x\) is one number \(f\), and the covariance reduces to
  \(\tau(1-\tau)s^2(X^\top X)^{-1}\) with the *sparsity* \(s=1/f(F^{-1}(\tau))\). The sparsity is the slope of the
  quantile function of the residuals, estimated over \(\tau\pm h\).
- *NID* (Hendricks and Koenker, 1992). The slope of the conditional quantile function in \(\tau\) is
  \(1/f_x\), so \(\hat f_i=2h/x_i^\top\bigl(\hat\beta(\tau+h)-\hat\beta(\tau-h)\bigr)\), clamped at zero, and the covariance is
  \(\tau(1-\tau)\hat H^{-1}\hat J\hat H^{-1}\) with \(\hat H=\sum_i\hat f_ix_ix_i^\top\) and \(\hat J=\sum_ix_ix_i^\top\). This allows the spread to depend on \(x\).
- *Bootstrap.* Resample the \(n\) rows with replacement, refit, and take the standard deviation of the
  refitted coefficients.

The bandwidth is Hall and Sheather's (1988)
\(h=n^{-1/3}z_{0.975}^{2/3}\bigl(1.5\,\phi(z_\tau)^2/(2z_\tau^2+1)\bigr)^{1/3}\) with \(z_\tau=\Phi^{-1}(\tau)\), the default of R's `quantreg`. Intervals
are estimate \(\pm\,t_{n-p,0.975}\) standard errors.

>> Goodness of fit

Squared-error \(R^2\) belongs to the mean. Koenker and Machado (1999) compare the loss with that of the
intercept-only fit at the same \(\tau\):

$$
R^1(\tau)=1-\frac{\sum_iw_i\rho_\tau\bigl(y_i-x_i^\top\hat\beta(\tau)\bigr)}{\sum_iw_i\rho_\tau(y_i-\hat q_\tau)} .
$$
\alttext(R 1 of tau equals 1 minus the ratio of the check loss of the fitted model to the check loss of the sample tau quantile.)

It lies in \([0,1]\) and measures the improvement at that quantile, not a share of variance.

>> In the library

`stats::QuantileRegression(data, "y ~ x1 + x2", {.quantile = tau})` resolves the formula like
`stats::LM` and fits by the simplex; `{.method = QuantileRegressionMethod::ADMM}` selects ADMM.
`basis()` gives the interpolated rows and `dual_solution()` the dual \(\hat a\). `coefficient_table(method,
level)` gives IID, NID or bootstrap standard errors with t tests and intervals.
`stats::quantile_process(data, formula, taus)` fits a grid of \(\tau\), and `check_loss`,
`hall_sheather_bandwidth` and `bofinger_bandwidth` are free functions.

> Simple examples

>> Five points by hand

Five points, \((1,1),(2,3),(3,2),(4,5),(5,4)\). A fit passes through two of them, so there are only ten
candidate lines, and the table lists every one with its check loss at \(\tau=0.25\) and \(\tau=0.5\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/qr_hand.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<double> x{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, y{1, 3, 2, 5, 4};
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", x);
  d.add_column("y", y);
  const auto loss = [&](double b0, double b1, double tau) {
    double total = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 5; ++i) total += dm::stats::check_loss(y[i] - b0 - b1 * x[i], tau);
    return total;
  };
  // Every line through two of the points. Its loss is linear in tau:
  // sum rho_tau(r_i) = tau * sum r_i + sum_{r_i < 0} |r_i| = A + B tau.
  std::vector<std::string> through;
  std::vector<double> b0s, b1s, as, bs, l25, l50;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 5; ++i)
    for (std::size_t j = i + 1; j < 5; ++j) {
      const double b1 = (y[j] - y[i]) / (x[j] - x[i]), b0 = y[i] - b1 * x[i];
      double sa = 0.0, sb = 0.0;
      for (std::size_t k = 0; k < 5; ++k) {
        const double r = y[k] - b0 - b1 * x[k];
        sb += r, sa += r < 0 ? -r : 0.0;
      }
      through.push_back(std::format("{} and {}", i + 1, j + 1));
      b0s.push_back(b0), b1s.push_back(b1), as.push_back(sa), bs.push_back(sb);
      l25.push_back(loss(b0, b1, 0.25)), l50.push_back(loss(b0, b1, 0.5));
    }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("line through points", through);
  table.add_column("intercept", b0s);
  table.add_column("slope", b1s);
  table.add_column("A", as);
  table.add_column("B", bs);
  table.add_column("loss, tau = 0.25", l25);
  table.add_column("loss, tau = 0.5", l50);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  std::print("{}\n\n", table.to_md(md));

  // The lower envelope of the ten loss lines A + B tau over 0 < tau < 1.
  std::size_t current = 0;
  for (std::size_t l = 1; l < as.size(); ++l)
    if (as[l] < as[current] - 1e-12 || (std::fabs(as[l] - as[current]) < 1e-12 && bs[l] < bs[current])) current = l;
  std::vector<std::pair<double, std::size_t>> pieces{{0.0, current}};
  for (double from = 0.0;;) {
    double next = 1.0;
    std::size_t to = current;
    for (std::size_t l = 0; l < as.size(); ++l)
      if (bs[l] < bs[current] - 1e-12) {
        const double t = (as[l] - as[current]) / (bs[current] - bs[l]);
        // At a tie between several crossings, the steepest line (smallest B) is the one below after it.
        if (t > from + 1e-12 && (t < next - 1e-12 || (t < next + 1e-12 && to != current && bs[l] < bs[to]))) next = t, to = l;
      }
    if (to == current) break;
    from = next, current = to;
    pieces.push_back({from, current});
  }
  std::vector<std::string> ranges;
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < pieces.size(); ++k) {
    const double lo = pieces[k].first, hi = k + 1 < pieces.size() ? pieces[k + 1].first : 1.0;
    const double mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
    const dm::stats::QuantileRegression fit(d, "y ~ x", {.quantile = mid});
    std::string rows;
    for (const std::size_t i : fit.basis()) rows += std::format("{}{}", rows.empty() ? "" : " and ", i + 1);
    ranges.push_back(std::format("{:g} < tau < {:g}", lo, hi));
    std::string tied;
    for (std::size_t l = 0; l < as.size(); ++l)
      if (l != pieces[k].second && std::fabs(as[l] - as[pieces[k].second]) < 1e-12 && std::fabs(bs[l] - bs[pieces[k].second]) < 1e-12)
        tied += std::format(" (tied at every tau with the line through {})", through[l]);
    std::print("- \\({:.4f}<\\tau<{:.4f}\\): line through {}{}; the simplex at \\(\\tau={:.4f}\\) passes through {}.\n", lo, hi,
               through[pieces[k].second], tied, mid, rows);
  }
  std::print("\n");
  for (const double tau : {0.25, 0.5}) {
    const dm::stats::QuantileRegression fit(d, "y ~ x", {.quantile = tau});
    std::string duals;
    for (const double a : fit.dual_solution()) duals += std::format("{}{:.4g}", duals.empty() ? "" : ", ", a);
    std::print("- \\(\\tau={}\\): intercept {:.4f}, slope {:.4f}, loss {:.4f}; dual \\(\\hat a=({})\\); {} pivots; "
               "certified optimal: {}.\n",
               tau, fit.coefficients()[0], fit.coefficients()[1], fit.objective(), duals, fit.iterations(),
               fit.converged() ? "yes" : "no");
  }

  // Left: the loss of a constant q (no slope) at three levels. Right: the optimal lines and their tau ranges.
  const dm::RGB ink{17, 24, 39}, blue{37, 99, 235}, red{220, 38, 38}, grey{148, 163, 184};
  const std::vector<std::pair<double, dm::RGB>> levels{{0.25, blue}, {0.5, ink}, {0.75, red}};
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (const auto& [tau, colour] : levels) {
    std::vector<double> qs, ls;
    for (double q = 0.0; q <= 6.0 + 1e-9; q += 0.01) {
      double total = 0.0;
      for (const double v : y) total += dm::stats::check_loss(v - q, tau);
      qs.push_back(q), ls.push_back(total);
    }
    const dm::stats::QuantileRegression constant(d, "y ~ 1", {.quantile = tau});
    left.lines(qs, ls, std::format("tau = {}", tau), colour, 2.0)
        .points({constant.coefficients()[0]}, {constant.objective()}, colour, 9.0);
  }
  left.x_limits(0.0, 6.0).y_limits(0.0, 16.0).title("Loss of a constant fit q").x_label("q").y_label("sum of check losses");
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  right.points(x, y, "", grey, 9.0);
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> piece_colours{blue, ink, red, dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, dm::RGB{147, 51, 234}};
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < pieces.size(); ++k) {
    const std::size_t l = pieces[k].second;
    right.lines({0.5, 5.5}, {b0s[l] + 0.5 * b1s[l], b0s[l] + 5.5 * b1s[l]}, ranges[k], piece_colours[k % 5], 2.0);
  }
  right.x_limits(0.5, 5.5).y_limits(-1.0, 9.0).title("Optimal lines over tau").x_label("x").y_label("y");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(left);
  fig.add(right);
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/qr_hand.svg");
}
```
